Directory "no-such-dir" does not exist.

Usage:
    convert-audio [options] <inputDir> <outputDir>
Video files are ingested as well: their audio stream is extracted and converted
like any other input.
Options:
    -h, --help      Print this help page.
    -m, --mono      Force mono output.
                    Default false
    -u, --surround  Keep a surround source's channels, up to 7.1, instead of
                    downmixing it to stereo.
                    Default false
    -a, --adaptive  Adaptive mode: decide channels and bitrate per file rather
                    than globally. The output keeps the source's own channel
                    count - capped at stereo unless -u is given - and its target
                    bitrate is the spoken-word (commentary) figure listed for
                    that channel count in the shared bitrate table, so a mono
                    source encodes mono at the lower rate, a stereo one stays
                    stereo, and with -u a multi-channel one gets its own rate.
                    Cannot be combined with -m or -b, and disables long-file
                    splitting.
                    Default false
    -k, --keep      Keep temporary files.
                    Default false
    -c, --copy-others
                    Copy non transcoded files from <inputDir> into <outputDir>
    -e, --encoding <codec>
                    Output encoding: opus or xheaac.
                    xheaac needs an external encoder (exhale) and is
                    written as .m4a.
                    Default opus
    -b, --bitrate <bitrate>
                    Bitrate of the output files
                    Default 46 kbps or 32 kbps for forced mono output, and
                    with -u the 46 scaled up with the channel count for a
                    surround source
    -j, --jobs <jobs>
                    Run up to <jobs> encoder processes in parallel.
                    Default one per CPU thread
